'''Ruan Xian''' ([[Floruit|fl.]] 3rd century), [[courtesy name]] '''Zhongrong''', was a Chinese scholar who lived in the [[Six Dynasties]] period. One of the [[Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove]], he was a skilled player of the Chinese lute, an old version of [[pipa]] which has been called ''[[Ruan (instrument)|ruan]]'' after his name since the [[Tang dynasty]]. His achievement in music reached such high as to be described as "divine understanding" in the ''[[Book of Jin]]''. Ruan Xian had a [[Xianbei]] slave who gave birth to his son, Ruan Fu.<ref>{{cite book|title=Zhou Mi's record of clouds and mist passing before one's eyes: an annotated translation|edition=illustrated, annotated|last1=Zhou|first1=Mi|first2=Ankeney|last2=Weitz|editor-last1=Ankeney|editor-last2=Weitz|year=2002|publisher=Brill|volume=54 of Sinica Leidensia|page=85|isbn=90-04-12605-8|quote=331 Ruan Fu (278-326) was the son of Ruan Xian (one of the Seven Worthies of the Bamboo Grove) by a Xianbei slave. Ruan Fu's uncommon obsession for wooden clogs (ji) caused him to exhautst himself in waxing his many pairs.}}</ref>

